 "requested_at": "2026-11-12T19:52:21.671967Z",
 "text": "The clause is **not enforceable** as written, because it allows notice by phone.\n\nSection 26 of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (S.O. 2006, c. 17) governs a landlord's entry into a rental unit. Entry for repairs is permitted only with the tenant's consent at the time of entry, in an emergency, or where the landlord has given proper notice and the entry falls between 8 a.m. and 8 p.m. The clause's time window (8 a.m. to 8 p.m.) and its 24-hour advance period match section 26. Its method of notice does not.\n\nSection 27 requires that the notice be given in writing and state the purpose and the date and time of entry. A phone call does not meet that requirement. The landlord therefore cannot rely on the clause to enter on telephone notice alone. Under section 3, a lease term that purports to waive or override the Act's protections is void, so the term does not change the statutory requirements. The clause would be enforceable only if the landlord gave written notice meeting section 27, or if the tenant consented at the time of entry.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 27",
